-- title:
--   Igusa's transitivity lemma on elements of order M
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a natural number, assumed nonzero, and let $H$ be a subgroup of the group $\mathrm{AddAut}(\mathbb{Z}/M \times \mathbb{Z}/M)$ of additive automorphisms of the abelian group $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^2$. Two hypotheses are imposed on $H$. First, for all $v, w \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^2$ whose additive orders both equal $M$, there exist $h \in H$ and an integer $n$ with $h(v) = n \cdot w$; that is, $H$ carries any element of order $M$ into the cyclic subgroup generated by any other such element. Second, there exist $t \in H$ and an integer $\varepsilon$ with $\varepsilon = 1$ or $\varepsilon = -1$ such that $t(1,0) = \varepsilon \cdot (1,0)$ and $t(0,1) = \varepsilon \cdot (1,1)$, so that $t$ is the matrix $\pm\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}$ in the standard basis. The conclusion is that $H$ is transitive on elements of order $M$: given $v, w \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^2$ with additive orders both equal to $M$, there exists $h \in H$ with $h(v) = w$.
--
--   This is the group-theoretic step in Igusa's determination of the Galois action on the $M$-torsion of an elliptic curve with transcendental $j$-invariant, stated additively and with the sign ambiguity built into the transvection hypothesis; in that application $H$ is the image of the absolute Galois group of the base field in $\mathrm{Aut}(E[M]) \cong \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/M)$, the first hypothesis coming from irreducibility of the modular equation and the second from local monodromy at $j = \infty$. It is used in [`WeierstrassCurve.exists_algEquiv_map_eq_of_addOrderOf_eq_of_transcendental_j`](thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.exists_algEquiv_map_eq_of_addOrderOf_eq_of_transcendental_j).
